   ['The cerebrum, the largest part of the brain, has two hemispheres with an outer cortex of grey matter. Its lobes: frontal (planning, decision-making, voluntary movement in the primary motor cortex, speech production in Broca\'s area), parietal (touch and body sensation), temporal (hearing, memory, language comprehension in Wernicke\'s area) and occipital (vision). Each hemisphere mainly controls the opposite side of the body.',
    'The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance and posture. The diencephalon includes the thalamus (relay station for sensory information) and the hypothalamus (temperature, hunger, thirst and hormone control). The brainstem (midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata) controls vital functions: the medulla regulates breathing, heart rate and blood pressure.',
    'The brain and spinal cord are protected by bone, three meninges (dura mater, arachnoid mater and pia mater) and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), which cushions them; meningitis is infection of the meninges. The spinal cord carries ascending sensory and descending motor tracts and coordinates reflexes. In a reflex arc (receptor → sensory neuron → interneuron in the spinal cord → motor neuron → effector), such as pulling the hand from a hot object, the response happens before the brain is aware.'],
   ['Frontal: planning, movement, Broca\'s area', 'Temporal: hearing, Wernicke\'s area; occipital: vision', 'Cerebellum: coordination; medulla: vital centres', 'Reflex arc: receptor → sensory → interneuron → motor → effector'],
